# 5. Calculation Engine

The calculation engine must be implemented as a separate backend business-logic module.

It must not place pricing calculations directly inside controllers or frontend components.

## 5.1 Deck Area Calculation

For rectangular decks:

Deck Area = Length × Width

Example:

Length = 24 ft  
Width = 16 ft

Deck Area = 24 × 16 = 384 sq.ft.

For square decks, use the same formula.

For L-shaped and custom decks, use the approved area captured in Phase 4 or a separately validated measurement breakdown.

Do not assume every deck is rectangular.

## 5.2 Material Quantity Calculation

General formula:

Required Material Quantity = Required Coverage Area ÷ Coverage per Unit

If an approved waste allowance exists:

Adjusted Material Quantity = Required Quantity × (1 + Waste Percentage / 100)

Round quantities according to the material's purchasing unit and approved business rules.

Example using illustrative values only:

- Deck Area: 384 sq.ft.
- Product Coverage: 200 sq.ft. per gallon.
- Waste Allowance: 10%.

Required Quantity:

384 ÷ 200 = 1.92 gallons

Adjusted Quantity:

1.92 × 1.10 = 2.112 gallons

If the product can only be purchased in whole gallons, the purchase quantity would be rounded according to the approved purchasing rule.

## 5.3 Material Cost Calculation

Material Cost = Required Purchase Quantity × Approved Unit Cost

Example:

Purchase Quantity = 3 gallons  
Cost per gallon = $50

Material Cost = 3 × $50 = $150

These values are illustrative and must not become production defaults.

## 5.4 Service Cost Calculation

Service costs must support the measurement method configured for each service.

Examples:

- Per square foot.
- Per linear foot.
- Per hour.
- Per unit.
- Fixed charge.

General formula:

Service Cost = Service Quantity × Approved Service Rate

For fixed charges:

Service Cost = Approved Fixed Charge

The system must prevent incompatible measurement units from being used in calculations.

## 5.5 Labor Cost Calculation

Only implement labor calculations after approved labor pricing rules are supplied.

Possible formula:

Labor Cost = Labor Quantity × Approved Labor Rate

The labor quantity and measurement method must be explicitly defined for each applicable service.

Do not invent labor hours or labor rates.

## 5.6 Additional Charges

Additional charges must be separately identified.

Examples:

- Travel.
- Equipment.
- Special preparation.

The calculation engine must not add a charge twice if it is already included in another pricing component.

## 5.7 Subtotal

Project Subtotal =

Material Costs  
+ Service Costs  
+ Labor Costs  
+ Approved Additional Charges

## 5.8 Minimum Job Price

If an approved minimum job price applies:

Pre-Tax Project Total = Maximum(Project Subtotal, Minimum Job Price)

The estimate must clearly distinguish the calculated subtotal from any minimum-price adjustment.

The exact commercial treatment must be confirmed before implementation.

## 5.9 Tax

If tax rules are approved:

Tax Amount = Applicable Taxable Amount × Tax Rate

Final Estimate = Pre-Tax Project Total + Tax Amount

The applicable tax basis and location rules must be approved by the business.

## 5.10 Final Estimate

The final estimate must provide a complete breakdown:

- Material Cost.
- Service Cost.
- Labor Cost.
- Additional Charges.
- Minimum Price Adjustment, if applicable.
- Subtotal.
- Tax.
- Final Estimated Amount.

Do not include Phase 9 contractor discounts or commissions in this phase.

# 6. Estimate Management

Create a dedicated Estimate Management module.

## 6.1 Estimate Creation

Users should be able to:

1. Select an existing deck.
2. Review deck measurements and project details.
3. Select compatible materials.
4. Select required services.
5. Review the calculated quantities.
6. Generate the estimate.
7. Save the estimate.

## 6.2 Estimate Information

Each estimate should contain:

- Estimate Number.
- Deck ID.
- Owner ID.
- Created By.
- Selected Materials.
- Selected Services.
- Material Quantity Breakdown.
- Material Cost Breakdown.
- Service Cost Breakdown.
- Labor Breakdown, if applicable.
- Additional Charges.
- Tax Breakdown, if applicable.
- Subtotal.
- Final Estimated Amount.
- Pricing Configuration Version.
- Estimate Status.
- Created Date.
- Updated Date.

## 6.3 Estimate Status

Suggested statuses:

- Draft.
- Generated.
- Revised.
- Approved.
- Rejected.
- Expired.

Confirm the business workflow before implementing approval-related transitions.

## 6.4 Estimate History

Users must be able to:

- View previous estimates.
- Open estimate details.
- Identify revised estimates.
- Compare estimate versions.
- Track creation and modification dates.

Do not silently overwrite an existing finalized estimate.

## 6.5 Estimate Versioning

When pricing rules or deck information change, the system must preserve the original estimate snapshot.

A revised estimate should create a new version or revision record.

Historical estimates must retain their original calculated values, rates, and pricing configuration references.

